---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import Lesson from "../../layouts/Lesson.astro";
import Mixup from "../../components/Mixup.astro";
import Scenario from "../../components/Scenario.astro";
import type {
  CheckQuestion,
  Scenario as ScenarioData,
} from "../../data/learn/types";
import { codeThemes } from "../../lib/code-themes.mjs";

/**
 * Three things a tool in the middle might send to the API behind it. The fuller
 * comparison (five ways, five questions) is LastHop on the Concepts page, and the
 * exchange itself is data/flows/token-exchange.ts; nothing here may contradict them.
 */
const chain: ScenarioData = {
  id: "chain",
  legend: "The tool sends the calendar API",
  cast: [
    { label: "Alice", role: "human" },
    { label: "Agent", sub: "your app", role: "agent" },
    { label: "Scheduling tool", sub: "in the middle", role: "agent" },
    { label: "Calendar API", sub: "the last hop", role: "resource" },
  ],
  asks: "What the calendar API can tell",
  questions: [
    "Is this for Alice?",
    "Which software is calling?",
    "Was this issued for me?",
  ],
  options: [
    {
      id: "forward",
      name: "The token it was handed",
      hops: [
        "“Find a time for us to meet”",
        "a token for the tool",
        "the same token again",
      ],
      exhibits: [
        {
          caption: "What arrives at the calendar API",
          lang: "json",
          text: `{
  "sub": "alice",
  "client_id": "calendar-assistant",
  "aud": "https://scheduler.example",
  "scope": "meetings.schedule"
}`,
        },
      ],
      findings: [
        { verdict: "yes", note: "The token names her." },
        {
          verdict: "no",
          note: "The token names the agent’s app. The tool in the middle is invisible.",
        },
        {
          verdict: "no",
          note: "`aud` is the scheduling tool. The calendar API has to ignore the address to accept it.",
        },
      ],
      so: `This is the tempting one: the tool already holds a token, so why not use it? Because it only works if the calendar API stops checking who a token is for, and then **any service that is ever handed a token from that issuer can use it there.** It is called token passthrough, and MCP forbids it.`,
    },
    {
      id: "key",
      name: "Its own API key",
      hops: [
        "“Find a time for us to meet”",
        "a token for the tool",
        "the tool’s key, and a name",
      ],
      exhibits: [
        {
          caption: "What arrives at the calendar API",
          lang: "http",
          text: `GET /events?day=tomorrow HTTP/1.1
Host: calendar.example
X-API-Key: sk_scheduler_41c0…
X-User: alice`,
        },
      ],
      findings: [
        {
          verdict: "no",
          note: "`alice` is only the tool’s word. The key is the same for every user.",
        },
        {
          verdict: "partly",
          note: "The tool, by its key. Not the agent behind it.",
        },
        { verdict: "yes", note: "The calendar API issued the key itself." },
      ],
      so: `The first lesson again, one hop further along. The calendar API sees the tool and nobody else, so the tool needs access to everyone’s calendar, and **the only thing keeping Alice’s request inside Alice’s calendar is the tool’s own code.**`,
    },
    {
      id: "exchange",
      name: "A new token, made for this hop",
      hops: [
        "“Find a time for us to meet”",
        "a token for the tool",
        "a new token: for Alice, by the tool",
      ],
      exhibits: [
        {
          caption: "What arrives at the calendar API",
          lang: "json",
          text: `{
  "sub": "alice",
  "aud": "https://calendar.example",
  "scope": "calendar.read",
  "act": { "sub": "scheduling-tool" }
}`,
        },
      ],
      findings: [
        { verdict: "yes", note: "`sub` is still Alice." },
        {
          verdict: "yes",
          note: "A second name has been added: `act`, the tool acting for her.",
        },
        { verdict: "yes", note: "`aud` is the calendar API." },
      ],
      so: `The tool takes the token it was handed back to the authorization server and asks for a new one, for the next hop. The server decides whether this tool may act for Alice at the calendar API, and if so says exactly that in a fresh token. **Each hop ends up holding a token that works in one place.**`,
      link: {
        href: "/flows/token-exchange/",
        text: "The exchange, step by step",
      },
    },
  ],
};

const checks: CheckQuestion[] = [
  {
    id: "next-hop",
    question:
      "A tool receives a token whose audience is the tool. To finish the job it has to call a billing API for the same user. What should it do?",
    options: [
      {
        text: "Send the billing API the token it received, since it is about the same user.",
        why: "That token is addressed to the tool. The billing API should refuse it, and if it does not, any service holding a token can reuse it elsewhere.",
      },
      {
        text: "Trade the token for a new one whose audience is the billing API.",
        correct: true,
        why: "A new token for each hop: still about the user, addressed to the next API, and no wider than this step needs.",
      },
      {
        text: "Use an administrator key, since the user was already checked.",
        why: "Then the billing API cannot tell which user the request is for, and the tool can reach everyone’s data.",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "three-am",
    question:
      "The calendar API’s log says Alice deleted a meeting at 3 a.m. Alice was asleep. An agent did it. What kind of token produces a log like that?",
    options: [
      {
        text: "One that names only Alice.",
        correct: true,
        why: "That is impersonation. The API cannot tell software from the person, so everything is recorded as hers.",
      },
      {
        text: "One that names Alice and the software acting for her.",
        why: "That is delegation, and the log would show both: Alice’s meeting, deleted by the tool acting for her.",
      },
      {
        text: "The tool’s own API key.",
        why: "Then the log would show the tool and would not mention Alice at all.",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "shrink",
    question:
      "As a request passes down a chain of tools, what should happen to the access each one holds?",
    options: [
      {
        text: "It should stay the same, so that nothing breaks.",
        why: "Then the last service in the chain can do everything the first could, including things this request never needed.",
      },
      {
        text: "It should grow, since later tools may need more.",
        why: "A tool that can ask for more than it was handed can be used to get around the limits on the one before it.",
      },
      {
        text: "It should shrink: each hop gets only what its own step needs.",
        correct: true,
        why: "One audience and a narrower scope at every exchange. Whatever goes wrong at one hop is then limited to that hop.",
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Lesson
  slug="chains"
  lede="Agents call tools, and tools call other services. Every hop needs a credential, and what gets handed along decides what the last service in the chain can know."
  checks={checks}
  keep={[
    "A service never passes on a token that was issued for itself. **Exchange, never forward.**",
    "A forwarded token only works if the next API stops checking the audience, and then any token from that issuer works there.",
    "**Delegation** names the person and the software acting for them. **Impersonation** names only the person. Prefer delegation.",
    "Access should shrink as it travels down a chain. It should never grow.",
  ]}
  names={[
    ["Forwarding the token you were handed", "Token passthrough"],
    ["A service talked into misusing access it was lent", "Confused deputy"],
    ["Trading a token for a new one, for the next hop", "Token exchange"],
    ["A token that names the person and the software", "Delegation"],
    ["A token that names only the person", "Impersonation"],
    ["The software named as acting", "Actor"],
    ["What some vendors call the exchange", "On-behalf-of"],
  ]}
>
  <h2>One more hop</h2>
  <p>
    So far the agent has called the calendar API directly. Often something sits in between. Alice asks the agent to
    find a time for a meeting. The agent calls a scheduling tool, which in MCP would be a server. The tool needs to
    see Alice’s calendar, so it calls the calendar API.
  </p>
  <p>
    The agent has a token for the tool: Alice approved it, and its audience is the tool. Now the tool has to call the
    calendar API. What does it send?
  </p>
  <p>
    There are three things it could reach for. Before you look, guess which of them lets the calendar API answer all
    three of its questions.
  </p>

  <Scenario scenario={chain} glance="All three at a glance" />

  <h2>Why forwarding is the dangerous one</h2>
  <p>
    Forwarding feels harmless. It is the same user and the same request, and the token is right there. The harm is
    in what it requires of the calendar API: accepting a token that was addressed to someone else.
  </p>
  <p>
    An API that does that can be used by any service holding any token from the same authorization server. A tool
    you connected for one purpose can quietly use your token for another. And an API that accepts tokens that were
    not addressed to it can be steered, by any caller holding one, into acting with authority that caller should not have.
    Security people call a service misused that way a <strong>confused deputy</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The audience check from the token lesson is the defense, and it only works if no service ever asks another to
    skip it.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do instead</h2>
  <p>
    The tool asks for a token of its own for the next hop. It takes the token it was handed back to the
    authorization server and asks for a new one, addressed to the calendar API. The authorization server decides
    whether this tool may act for Alice there, and if so issues it. That trade is a{' '}
    <strong>token exchange</strong>, and this is what then arrives at the calendar API:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`{
  "sub": "alice",
  "aud": "https://calendar.example",
  "scope": "calendar.read",
  "act": { "sub": "scheduling-tool" }
}`}
    lang="json"
    themes={codeThemes as any}
    defaultColor={false}
  />
  <p>
    Still about Alice, now addressed to the calendar API, and no wider than this step needs. So the rule is short:{' '}
    <strong>exchange, never forward.</strong>
  </p>

  <h2>Two ways to write “for Alice”</h2>
  <p>A token made for the next hop can be written two ways.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      It can name <strong>only Alice</strong>. The calendar API then believes Alice is calling in person, and its
      logs will say so. This is <strong>impersonation</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      It can name <strong>Alice, and separately the software acting for her</strong>. This is{' '}
      <strong>delegation</strong>, and the second name is called the <strong>actor</strong>. It is the{' '}
      <code>act</code> line in the token above.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Delegation is the one to want. The API can apply Alice’s permissions and the tool’s limits together, block one
    misbehaving tool without locking Alice out, and say afterward what did what. With impersonation the log shows
    Alice doing things at three in the morning that Alice never did.
  </p>
  <p>
    One caution. Whether the new token carries an actor depends on the authorization server that issues it, and
    whether anything is done with it depends on the API. Check what yours actually issue and read before you rely on
    it.
  </p>

  <Mixup belief="Token exchange is how a tool gets more access than it was given.">
    <p>
      It is the reverse. An exchange should produce a token that does less: one audience, and a scope no wider
      than the next step needs. The authorization server can also say no. Access shrinks as it travels down a
      chain, so that whatever goes wrong at one hop stays at that hop.
    </p>
  </Mixup>

  <h2>Where you will meet this</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>An MCP server that calls another API.</strong> It must not pass along the token the client sent it.
      It gets a token of its own for the API behind it, by exchange or by asking the user to connect that account
      directly.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An agent that hands work to another agent.</strong> The same question one level up: what does the
      second agent receive, and what can the service behind it tell?
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A request that crosses into another company.</strong> Their authorization server does not know your
      users, so there are two trades: one at home for a short-lived grant, and one there for a token.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A request passed between services inside your own infrastructure.</strong> One variant makes a single
      exchange at the edge, for a short-lived token built to travel from service to service. What is never forwarded
      is a token addressed to someone else.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Lesson>
